= Computability =

== Overview ==
'''Computability''' studies which functions and problems can be solved by effective procedures under formal models of computation.

It asks what can be computed in principle, independently of whether the computation is practical.

== Computable functions ==
A function is Turing-computable when some [[Turing machine]] produces the correct output for every valid input and halts.

Equivalent models include lambda-definability and general recursive functions.

== Decision problems ==
A decision problem asks for a yes/no answer for inputs.

A problem is decidable if an algorithm halts with the correct answer on every input.

Some problems are undecidable.

== Halting problem ==
The halting problem asks whether an arbitrary program/machine will halt on an input.

No general algorithm can decide this correctly for all program/input pairs.

== Church–Turing thesis ==
The Church–Turing thesis identifies formal Turing-computability with the informal notion of effective calculability.

The thesis is supported by convergence among independent models and extensive practice, but it is not a formal theorem about an already formalized external concept.

== Computability and complexity ==
A problem can be computable yet require enormous resources.

[[Computational complexity]] studies resource requirements after computability has established that a solution procedure exists.

Therefore:

<pre>
computable ≠ efficiently computable
</pre>

